I'm working on a Rust project `stackrs` that implements an immutable linked list. I'm getting a borrow-checker error when trying to implement a method `fn push(&self, value: i32) -> List` that returns a new list with the value prepended. The code is in `src/lib.rs`. Here's the relevant snippet: 

```rust
pub enum List {
    Empty,
    Cons(i32, Box<List>),
}

impl List {
    pub fn push(&self, value: i32) -> List {
        match self {
            List::Empty => List::Cons(value, Box::new(List::Empty)),
            List::Cons(head, tail) => List::Cons(value, Box::new(self)),
        }
    }
}
```

The error I get is: `error[E0507]: cannot move out of `*self` which is behind a shared reference`. I need to understand why this happens and fix it. Please debug and provide the corrected code and explanation.

```rust
pub enum List {
    Empty,
    Cons(i32, Box<List>),
}

impl List {
    pub fn push(&self, value: i32) -> List {
        match self {
            List::Empty => List::Cons(value, Box::new(List::Empty)),
            List::Cons(head, tail) => List::Cons(value, Box::new(self)),
        }
    }
}
```

The error occurs because in the `List::Cons(head, tail)` arm, you're trying to move `self` (which is a reference) into `Box::new(self)`. You cannot move out of a borrowed value. Instead, you need to clone the existing list or rebuild it recursively. Since your list is immutable, the simplest fix is to derive `Clone` for `List` and clone `self` when building the new list. Here's the corrected code:

```rust
#[derive(Clone)]
pub enum List {
    Empty,
    Cons(i32, Box<List>),
}

impl List {
    pub fn push(&self, value: i32) -> List {
        match self {
            List::Empty => List::Cons(value, Box::new(List::Empty)),
            List::Cons(_, _) => List::Cons(value, Box::new(self.clone())),
        }
    }
}
```

This clones the existing list (which is cheap for a linked structure) and puts it in the new node. Now the method works without moving out of the reference. Alternatively, you could implement a recursive clone manually, but deriving `Clone` is idiomatic and clear.

